from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
import json,shutil
root=Path.cwd();site=root/'.lavish/prooflab-walkthrough';out=root/'outputs'
css=(root/'.lavish/hackathon-start/index.html').read_text().split('<style>',1)[1].split('</style>',1)[0]
css+='''
.hero{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.8fr) minmax(0,1fr)}.diagram-image{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border-radius:8px}.twocol{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:16px}.twocol>*{min-width:0}.story article{border-left:3px solid var(--blue);padding:8px 0 8px 20px;margin:20px 0}.story h3{font-size:20px}.story p:last-child{margin:0}.fact{color:var(--green);font-weight:650}.example{color:var(--amber);font-weight:650}.progress{font:14px var(--mono);color:var(--blue)}.files{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px 20px}.note{margin:18px 0}.contract{font-size:14px}section{scroll-margin-top:18px}
@media(max-width:760px){.hero,.twocol{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)}}
'''
d=json.loads((out/'prooflab-component-inventory.json').read_text())
rows=''.join(f'<tr><th scope="row">{x["id"]} · {escape(x["name"])}</th><td>{escape(x["purpose"])}</td></tr>' for x in d['groups'][0]['items'])
html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>ProofLab walkthrough · M01 telecom workflow</title><style>'''+css+'''</style></head><body><header class="top"><strong>QUEST FOR PROOF / COMPONENT WALKTHROUGH</strong><nav><a href="#story">The story</a><a href="#parts">M01 parts</a><a href="#handoff">What assurance needs</a><button id="theme" type="button">Change theme</button></nav></header><main class="wrap">
<section class="hero"><div><p class="progress">M01 / 16 · THE PROVIDED SUBJECT WORKFLOW</p><h1>Follow the fault.<br>Understand what we govern.</h1><p class="lead">M01 is the telecom system attempting to diagnose the customer’s problem. Our controls and evidence investigator observe or govern this work.</p><p>We will use the same approach for each major component: purpose, input, internal steps, output and connection to the next component. This chapter covers M01 only.</p></div><aside class="card recommended"><p class="kicker">Source boundary</p><p><span class="fact">Workshop-described:</span> three agents, two zone gateways, one shared model gateway, guardrails, telemetry and a digital twin/scenario switch.</p><p><span class="example">Illustrative:</span> the customer wording, ticket/run identifiers and any particular diagnosis in this walkthrough.</p><p class="muted">The source describes the intended lab. We have not inspected its running code or actual payloads.</p></aside></section>
<section><h2>Two kinds of communication.</h2><figure><img class="diagram-image" src="m01-flow.svg" alt="Customer agent passes work through Gateway A to IT triage, which passes work through Gateway B to network investigation. Findings return through the same zone sequence. All three agents use the shared model gateway for Nemotron model requests."><figcaption>Business handoffs cross gateways A/B. Model requests use the shared model gateway. These names describe different roles; the deployed products and hook APIs still need verification.</figcaption></figure><div class="twocol" style="margin-top:16px"><article class="card"><h3>An agent is the running workflow.</h3><p>It maintains task context and uses model responses and tools to decide its next step. A single agent can make several model or tool calls.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>The model and digital twin have different roles.</h3><p>Nemotron provides model inference. The digital twin supplies a network investigation/simulation capability. The twin does not automatically count as a fourth agent or independent ground-truth labels.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="story"><p class="kicker">Illustration based on the supplied flow</p><h2>“My internet connection keeps dropping.”</h2><div class="story"><article><h3>1 · Customer zone: receive the complaint.</h3><p>The ServiceNow customer/chat agent receives the permitted complaint and creates or works with the trouble ticket. Its job is to keep the customer interaction connected to an investigation.</p><p>We might call the ticket INC-001 and the fault-resolution run FM-001 for this example. These are different identities. Our trusted lifecycle integration establishes run context before the first governed action; the ticket link can be attached when the ticket exists. Actual field names and propagation belong to M03.</p></article><article><h3>2 · Gateway A: hand work to IT.</h3><p>The customer agent passes the investigation request into the IT zone through gateway A. A proposed handoff would carry the run identity, complaint and authorized customer/service references. That is our desired correlation contract, not a claim about the lab’s current JSON fields.</p></article><article><h3>3 · IT zone: establish account and service context.</h3><p>The AWS triage agent queries account records, subscriptions and relevant configuration. It determines the context the network investigation needs and sends it through gateway B.</p><p>A successful account lookup does not prove the fault has been found. The agent may call tools and Nemotron more than once; we need actual traces to learn the precise sequence.</p></article><article><h3>4 · Network zone: investigate the fault.</h3><p>The AWS/NVIDIA investigation agent uses the available network inventory and digital twin to investigate the problem. A diagnosis and suggested next action are possible outputs; the specific fault and result depend on the selected lab scenario.</p><p>A scenario switch can choose a lab situation. We must still establish an accepted label/measurement basis before using that situation to score Control 9.</p></article><article><h3>5 · Return the findings.</h3><p>The result travels back through gateway B to IT, then through gateway A to the customer zone. The customer agent can use it in its response or ticket workflow.</p><p>The sources do not establish automatic network repair, successful remediation or ticket closure. Each would require its own actual action and evidence if present.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="parts"><p class="kicker">Every M01 subcomponent</p><h2>The pieces inside this provided workflow.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Subcomponent</th><th>Responsibility / boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+rows+'''</tbody></table></div><p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">Inventory correction from rereading slide 17: supplied guardrails, scenario switch and telemetry collector/storage are now explicit as M01.10–M01.12. Existing IDs are unchanged; the total inventory now has 123 subcomponents. Sandboxes/credentials remain under M16.</p></section>
<section id="handoff"><p class="kicker">What M01 must expose to our assurance layer</p><h2>The business answer and its evidence are different outputs.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table class="contract"><thead><tr><th>Needed fact / record</th><th>Why our architecture needs it</th><th>Consumer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Authorized run, action and attempt identities</th><td>Connect actions across agents; distinguish retries and duplicate delivery.</td><td>M03 identity; M05 reconciliation</td></tr>
<tr><th>Expected work and actual lifecycle events</th><td>Measure recording coverage without treating surviving logs as the expected population.</td><td>M05 acquisition; M07 Control 7</td></tr>
<tr><th>Actual usage for each model execution</th><td>Reconcile spend across all three agents and every actual retry.</td><td>M05 reconciliation; M09 Control 16</td></tr>
<tr><th>Diagnosis outcome and scenario membership</th><td>Score against accepted labels and associate the run with a comparable window.</td><td>M08 Control 9</td></tr>
<tr><th>Notes, tool results and source references</th><td>Support cited model extraction and investigation of contradictions.</td><td>M06 workspace; M11 investigator</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><div class="note">This is the information our design requires. It is not proof that the running workshop exposes every field, expected population or interception hook. The existing telemetry collector preserves what arrives; that alone cannot prove that every required event arrived.</div></section>
<section><h2>Where our three controls become relevant.</h2><p><strong>Control 7:</strong> were the declared events across this whole workflow recorded, with acceptable timing?</p><p><strong>Control 16:</strong> how many tokens did this run actually consume across all agents and retries?</p><p><strong>Control 9:</strong> how did measured diagnostic performance in the applicable window compare with the frozen baseline?</p><p>The provided agents attempt to resolve the fault. The control implementations establish whether their declared requirements held. The Nemotron evidence investigator later helps assemble and explain the supporting records.</p></section>
<section class="card recommended"><p class="progress">NEXT · M02</p><h2>Catch the first governed action at a boundary.</h2><p>We will follow one model request from an agent into the shared model gateway, identify what the enforcement point can observe, and show exactly how our adapter invokes a control. We will keep observation-only and optional blocking capabilities distinct.</p></section>
<footer><p><strong>Sources:</strong> immersion-day deck, physical slide 17, visually inspected; corresponding supplied meeting-transcript passage; current V4 component inventory. This is a conceptual walkthrough, not a trace of a live run.</p><p><strong>Design source:</strong> existing Quest for Proof page tokens and layout. SVG rendered and visually checked; HTML and interaction script checked statically. No browser-rendering verification or runtime integration was performed.</p></footer>
</main><script>document.getElementById('theme').addEventListener('click',()=>{const r=document.documentElement;const dark=r.dataset.theme==='dark'||(!r.dataset.theme&&matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);r.dataset.theme=dark?'light':'dark';});</script></body></html>'''
(site/'index.html').write_text(html)
